  "narration": {
    "title": "The Award Boards: Who's Leading the Argument",
    "intro": "It's late June, so the award debates have stopped being theoretical. Our 0-100 Scores have front-runners in all six races, and two of them are running away. Here's where the conversation stands today.",
    "mvp_lead": "In the AL, Yordan Alvarez owns the MVP board at 85.8, hitting .322 with 25 home runs and a 1.069 OPS that sits above even his lofty career mark. Ben Rice (81.3) is the surprise chaser, giving the Yankees a first baseman who hits after years of sub-.730 work there. The NL is tighter: Kyle Schwarber leads at 80.0 on the strength of 29 homers, with Juan Soto a half-step back at 77.8 and the higher OPS at .965.",
    "cy_lead": "Jacob Misiorowski is the runaway, an 89.1 Score backed by a 1.45 ERA, a 0.75 WHIP and 138 strikeouts \u2014 and a 0.62 ERA over his last 29 innings. Cristopher S\u00e1nchez (83.9) keeps him honest in the NL. The AL is closer at the top: Cam Schlittler's 85.1 and 1.71 ERA edge Drew Rasmussen's 77.0.",
    "roy_lead": "Munetaka Murakami paces the AL rookies at 76.5 with 20 home runs and a .938 OPS, comfortably ahead of Kazuma Okamoto's 69.3. The NL is a logjam: Sal Stewart's 67.1 barely tops TJ Rumfield (66.7), who's hitting .284 with a .911 OPS over his last 30 days since arriving in a midseason trade. No clear front-runner there yet."
  }
